      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "If $\\cos x=\\frac{2}{11},$ then $-5\\cos (x+4\\pi )+4=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The period of $y=\\cos x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for an integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos x=\\cos (x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=2$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cos x=\\cos (x+2\\cdot 2\\cdot \\pi )=\\cos (x+4\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\cos x=\\frac{2}{11},$ then $\\cos (x+4\\pi )=\\frac{2}{11}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\cos (x+4\\pi )+4 & =-5(\\frac{2}{11})+4 \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{11}+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{34}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
